  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Locally heavy rain possible Sunday into Monday,\nwith an active weather pattern expected next week.\n\nHigh pressure departing east over the Atlantic today will bring the \nreturn of a warm southerly breeze across the region. Highs are \nforecast to reach the mid 80s to low 90s, and there is going to be a \nnoticeable increase in humidity. Still, brief mid-level ridging \naloft is going to keep a cap on convection today as only a few stray \nshowers are possible this afternoon.\n\nAttention turns to the low pressure system and its associated cold \nfront over the Midwest that slowly moves east across the OH Valley \nthis weekend into early next week. The base of the mid-level trough \nis forecast to pinch off over the lower OH Valley tonight, though \nstill remain attached to the parent upper trough. Through the start \nof next week the lobes of energy along the upper trough become more \nelongated, stretching south into the TN Valley, then stall near the \ncentral Appalachians during the early to middle part of next week. \nThe cold front also stalls over the area sometime early next week. \nTo our east, the Bermuda High builds into the western Atlantic and \nlikely remains in place through the end of next week.\n\nThe Mid-Atlantic, sandwiched between these two nearly stationary \nsynoptic features, will see a steady stream of deep tropical \nmoisture advecting into the region. Ample heat and humidity is going \nto produce rounds of showers and thunderstorms each day starting \nSunday, and possibly continuing through all of next week.\n\nThere is some potential for a few strong to severe thunderstorms \nSunday afternoon/evening, with SPC maintaining the Marginal Risk \n(Level 1 of 5) across most of the area. Damaging wind gusts look to \nbe the primary threat with any severe storm that develops. \nPrecipitable water values rise into the 2 to 2.25\" range, which will \nenhance the potential for heavy rainfall in any thunderstorm. The \n7/31 12Z and early 8/1 00Z ensemble guidance remain in flux \nregarding high-end rainfall amounts, so the rainfall forecast \nremains uncertain. Widespread amounts of 0.5\" to 1\" are most likely \nSunday into Monday, but a stripe of localized higher rain amounts \nare possible. It is hard to point to any specific area that is most \nat risk at this moment, so be sure to keep up with future forecast \nupdates. Since storm motion will be fast, any flood threat is going \nto come from repeated rounds of showers/thunderstorms. Flood Watches \nmay be needed if confidence increases, but does not seem likely at \nthis time.\n\nBeyond Monday, the greatest concentration of thunderstorms is likely \nbe over the terrain and in/around where the bay/river breeze sets \nup. Overall coverage remain scattered, but likely amplified during \nthe peak heating period given the high PWATS (1.5-2\"+) sitting over \nthe region. Any flood threat would remain relegated to poor drainage \nareas pending repetitive rounds of storms or if storm track/sit over \nan urban location. Highs in the upper 80s to 90F.",
